diff --git a/Submit-Flight-Plan.md b/Submit-Flight-Plan.md
index 1006f7d..a01c783 100644
--- a/Submit-Flight-Plan.md
+++ b/Submit-Flight-Plan.md
@@ -30,14 +30,18 @@ The **total time is filed rounded up to the next 5 minutes** — nobody files `0
 
 Aircraft and pilot details persist on the device. They sync through Google Drive only when you separately enable **Sync settings too**, so this is usually a one-time setup. The same details serve **both** plan types — the ICAO message and the cross-country form:
 
-- **Call sign** — three letters is enough; `4X-` is printed beside the field, not typed. Once the
+- **Call sign** — three letters is enough; `4X-` is printed beside the field, not typed. It takes
+  English capitals and digits only, as you type: lower case is raised, and with the Hebrew
+  keyboard still on each letter becomes the English key it sits on. Once the
   [aircraft](Flight-Plan#aircraft-inputs) in use has a call sign, it is that one, read-only here.
 - **Aircraft type** — the ICAO type of the flight plan's aircraft, **read-only**: chosen in the
   plan's **Aircraft** row (the type selected for the aircraft), with a line
   under it saying so. One place to choose it, and a filed type that cannot disagree with the
   figures the plan was worked out with.
-- **Fuel endurance** — in hours: the aircraft's usable fuel less the taxi allowance, over its
-  burn, rounded down to the half hour (see [Fuel](Fuel-Calculator)).
+- **Fuel endurance** — in hours: **5 h** by default, but never more than the aircraft's tanks
+  hold (usable fuel less the taxi allowance, over its burn, rounded down to the half hour — see
+  [Fuel](Fuel-Calculator)), so a C152 or a microlight files what it can fly. Your own choice is
+  kept once made.
 - **Persons on board** — as "pilot + n".
 - **Pilot in command, licence number, mobile** — in **Latin letters**. The message is an ASCII telex; Hebrew would arrive as mojibake. The licence and mobile fields ask for digits, so a phone offers its keypad rather than the full keyboard.
 - **Your email** — required, for **both** plan types. The approval comes back by mail, and if you are filing from a machine whose mail account is not yours it would otherwise reach that machine's owner. Your address is copied (CC) on the message — on the ICAO plan, and on the mail the cross-country form opens to the FPL desk.
@@ -217,6 +221,7 @@ for things the plan cannot know:
 Everything else is written from the plan you have already filled in — registration, type,
 pilot, licence, mobile, persons on board, both aerodromes and the date. The message is shown
 in full and **you can edit it**; once you do, changing the options above stops rewriting it.
+Anything still to fill in shows as a blank in the message's own language (`[ date / time ]`).
 Times carry the clock they are on (`19:30 LT (16:30Z)`), since you type local and the desk
 reads Zulu.
 
